<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucretia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucretia was, according to Roman tradition, a noblewoman whose rape by Sextus Tarquinius, son of the king, and her subsequent suicide provoked the overthrow of the Roman monarchy in 509 BCE. Her story, told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, became a celebrated subject of later literature and art, though historians regard it as legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucretia.jpg" alt="Lucretia" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artemisia Gentileschi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artemisia_Gentileschi_-_Lucretia_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>No source of her own time survives for Lucretia or for the rape. What is known comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and from Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who wrote about five hundred years afterwards, and modern writers count her story as part of Roman mythohistory. The evidence is taken to point to a real woman named Lucretia and to an event that played a critical part in the fall of the monarchy, but the details vary with the writer. Dionysius puts the rape and her death at 508 BCE, setting it in the year of the first of the Roman fasti, and historians accept a range of about five years.</p><p>She was the daughter of the magistrate Spurius Lucretius and wife of Lucius Tarquinius Collatinus. Livy and Dionysius present the marriage as the Roman ideal and Lucretia as a model for Roman girls. During the siege of Ardea, the story goes, the king's sons and their kinsmen, including Brutus and Collatinus, disputed whose wife was the most virtuous. Riding home, they found the other women socialising, but Lucretia at home alone, working her wool.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sextus Tarquinius, son of the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Lucius Tarquinius Superbus</a>, entered her room at night and raped her, in one version after threatening to kill her and a slave and claim he had found them in adultery. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s account she sent for her father and her husband, each to bring a friend as witness, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a>. The men told her that the mind sins, not the body, and that where there was no consent there was no guilt. After exacting an oath of vengeance she stabbed herself in the heart.</p><p>Brutus swore on the bloody dagger to drive out the Tarquins. The body was shown in the Forum, the assembly of the curiae voted to end the monarchy, and Brutus and Collatinus were chosen as the first consuls. The story was retold by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, by Augustine in The City of God, by Chaucer and by Shakespeare in his 1594 poem The Rape of Lucrece, and since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> her suicide has been a subject for artists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, Dürer, Botticelli and Artemisia Gentileschi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucretia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The traditional date is roughly confirmed by the treaty between Rome and Carthage that Polybius dated to the first year of the Republic, though the details of the story, including the rape of <b>Lucretia</b>, are legend.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Roman tradition recorded by Livy, the Romans expelled their last king, Tarquinius Superbus, in 509 BCE, after his son raped the noblewoman <b>Lucretia</b>, and Lucius Junius Brutus and Lucretia’s husband Collatinus became the first consuls.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition says that after Sextus Tarquinius raped <b>Lucretia</b>, Lucius Junius Brutus led the nobles in expelling Tarquinius Superbus in 509 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-535" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Lucretia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-535">What was here in 535 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
